WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.540
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:10.210
Have a look at this magnificent idea: the
input is a digital 3D model of an object and

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:16.029
a viewpoint of our choice, and the output
is an easy to follow, step by step breakdown

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:17.330
on how to draw it.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.410
Automated drawing tutorials!

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.779
I wish tools like this were available back
when I was a child!

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:24.779
Awesome!

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:29.179
This technique offers a way to create the
scaffoldings to help achieving the correct

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:34.770
perspective and positioning for the individual
elements of the 3D model, something that novice

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.090
artists often struggle with.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.670
This problem is particularly challenging,
because we have a bunch of competing solutions

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.449
and we have to decide which one should be
presented to the user.

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:50.949
To achieve this, we have to include a sound
mathematical description of how easy a drawing

00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:52.160
process is.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.920
The algorithm also makes adjustments to the
individual parts of the model to make them

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.340
easier to draw without introducing severe
distortions to the shapes.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.700
The proposed technique uses graph theory to
find a suitable ordering of the drawing steps.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.020
Beyond the scientific parts, there are a lot
of usability issues to be taken into consideration:

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.130
for instance, the algorithm should notify
the user when a given guide is not to be used

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.500
anymore and can be safely erased.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.369
Novice, apprentice, and adept users are also
to be handled differently.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:29.729
To show the validity of this solution, the
authors made a user study where they tested

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:35.030
this new tutorial type against the most common
existing solution, and found that the users

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:39.890
were not only able to create more accurate
drawings with it, but they were also enjoying

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.170
the process more.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:47.049
I commend the authors for taking into consideration
the overall experience of the drawing process,

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:51.600
which is an incredibly important factor - if
the user enjoyed the process, he'll surely

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.289
come back for more, and of course, the more
we show up, the more we learn.

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:00.659
Some of these tutorials are available on the
website of the authors, as always, I've linked

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.189
it in the video description.

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:06.030
If you are in the mood to draw, make sure
to give it a go and let us know how it went

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.250
in the comments section!

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.360
Hell, even I am now in the mood to give this
a try!

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.840
If I disappear for a while, you know where
I am.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.580
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

